\section{Labels and numbers}{


Several regions can share a label: a section has many airways. Each is
numbered as it is saved (\code{airway_01}, \code{airway_02}, ...), so the report can
treat them as independent regions or pool them per sample. The prompt lists
the labels already used anywhere in the study (every \code{roi_labels.csv} beside
this one in \code{data_path}), so the same thing is called the same name in every
acquisition. Type the label only; the number is added for you.

A pixel belongs to at most one region. A region drawn over part of an
earlier one takes those pixels, and an earlier region left with no pixels is
dropped. Tissue outside every region is \code{unassigned}, which the report can
keep or leave out.
}

\section{The file}{


Regions are saved as \code{roi_labels.csv} in the acquisition's \code{.raw} folder,
one per acquisition, beside \code{tissue_pixels.csv} -- which is not touched. It
lists the labelled pixels by \code{x}, \code{y}, \code{roi_label} and \code{roi_id}, and is
written after every region, so nothing is lost if the session ends early.
Running \code{labelROIs()} again adds regions to the file; \code{overwrite = TRUE}
starts again, keeping the old file as a dated backup.

Panels acquired over the same area share their pixel grid, so the regions
drawn on one apply to the others: \code{copy_to} writes the same file into their
folders. Regions are matched by pixel position, as the tissue mask is.
}

